WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.480
Linux file security is built around ownership and permissions.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:09.220
A file has an owner, a group, and a file mode that describes who can read, write,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:10.720
or execute it.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.020
That model is simple enough to learn,

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.520
but important enough to affect almost every Linux system.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.380
Incorrect permissions can expose sensitive data, allow unauthorized changes,

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.200
prevent applications from working, or make troubleshooting much harder.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:30.140
Read access matters because many files contain useful or sensitive information:

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:36.200
configuration, credentials, logs, application data, scripts, reports, keys,

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.100
and system details.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.600
Write access matters because changing the wrong file can alter behavior, damage data,

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.340
or weaken controls.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.640
Execute access matters because scripts and programs should run only where that makes

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:50.740
sense.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.080
A file that is readable or writable by too many users may be a security problem even if

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.900
the system appears healthy.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.520
Directories need special attention.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:05.780
Directory permissions affect whether users can list names, traverse paths, create files,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.200
remove files, or reach objects below the directory.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.060
A sensitive file can still be exposed if the path around it is poorly controlled.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.160
Application directories, shared folders, log locations, temporary spaces,

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.820
and deployment paths should be reviewed with their real purpose in mind.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.080
The defensive habit is to match permissions to need.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.940
Sensitive files should not be broadly accessible.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.720
Scripts should be controlled by the right owner and group.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:36.740
Logs should be available for operations and investigation without unnecessary exposure.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.440
Before changing permissions on a production system,

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:44.940
administrators should understand the application, test safely, document the change,

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:46.600
and keep a rollback plan.
